- **A brand-new session on this repo alone has no git read access to
  <shared-set-repo-name> or <individual-repo-name>** — session repo access is
  scoped per session, never inherited just because `precedent.json` or a
  bootstrap hook names a repo. No token or secret is involved: the tool
  that grants a session this access rides the same GitHub identity already
  behind the session, so it only ever succeeds because that person is
  already a collaborator on the target repo. **At the start of every session, attach each practice source session
  start reports missing -- the sets `precedent.json` declares and those
  the person's individual set brings -- silently, with no confirmation
  needed first.** Session start prints the exact `add_repo` call for
  each one, in the spelling `precedent.json` declares: use that spelling
  for the clone too, whatever case the reply answers in, and never
  `register_repo_root` a source (attach-never-clone-individual); when the environment's token already cloned them all it
  prints none, and the session attaches nothing. Attach a source that is
  already cloned only when this session has to push to it. The settings
  Precedent installs allow the attach and the clone, so neither stops
  for a prompt. This is a read-only
  scope extension, not a credential or production change. Never write this
  as "if the clone fails, ask to add the repo" — that only works when a
  human happens to be watching for the failure, and the whole point is
  that a person only ever attaches the one repo they're actually working
  in; reaching for the others is this session's own job, every time.
  **Attach <individual-repo-name>, and keep one clone of it** (practice
  `attach-never-clone-individual`). The attach tool's reply says to clone
  it to `/home/user/<name>`. If the session-start hook got there first,
  that path is already a link to its clone and the clone command stops
  with "already exists": use the path as it is. If not, clone it there;
  the next bootstrap run points `~/.config/precedent/config.json` at that
  clone and links `~/precedent-individual` to it. Either path is the same
  tree. Never clone it anywhere else: a second copy is one nothing loads,
  and it drifts from the first within the hour. <shared-set-repo-name> is
  the other way round: its clone lives at the path `precedent.json`
  resolves, beside this repo, so clone it there if nothing has.


## Git / workflow

- Develop on a feature branch; open a PR; merge only when the user says so.
- **Start every thread by merging latest `origin/<default-branch>` into your
  branch**; avoid two concurrent threads editing the same <conflict-prone
  file class>.
- **Merging a branch follows practice `merge-runbook`**, which the merge
  gate prints: the capture and export gates first, conflicts resolved by
  file class, and `<your audit commands>` plus
  `python3 tools/precedent_sync_views.py --repo . --check` passing before
  the merge commits.

## Practice sources — Precedent loader (policy)

- `precedent.json` declares every practice source in force here; INSTALL.md
  §0 has the resolution and precedence rules. The `universal` source is a
  **real vendored copy** at the tracked path its entry names (recommended:
  `precedent/universal/`), so every collaborator and fresh container has it
  without a sibling checkout. A `team` source resolves live from a sibling
  clone and is never vendored.
- **If this repo and the universal source's upstream are both public,**
  nothing proprietary may appear under the vendored universal path. No
  script enforces that in the loader model yet, so review changes there by
  hand.
- `python3 tools/precedent_sync_views.py --repo . --check` is this repo's
  drift gate: run it before trusting this file's generated block, and after
  a vendored copy or `precedent.json` changes.
- Export gate: practice `practice-export-loop`.
